│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ full                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ regime_rank_xgb                                                                           │
│ patch_description        │ I’m switching from “raw indicator stacking” to a more regime-invariant feature pipeline   │
│                          │ built around short-horizon, rolling-normalized, and percentile-ranked signals. The idea   │
│                          │ is to reduce dependence on absolute price/volatility levels that broke in the later       │
│                          │ regime, and instead feed XGBoost compact, stable representations of market state and      │
│                          │ market-state change.                                                                      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ Compared with the prior programs, this version:                                           │
│                          │ - emphasizes 5–20 bar lookbacks instead of longer decay-prone windows,                    │
│                          │ - converts several core signals into rolling z-scores / ranks,                            │
│                          │ - adds explicit change-of-regime features like volatility trend, efficiency trend, and    │
│                          │ autocorrelation trend,                                                                    │
│                          │ - includes multiplicative interactions only where they are likely to help generalization, │
│                          │ - loosens model regularization a bit so predictions do not collapse toward the mean.      │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The output interface is unchanged: `build_features(df)` returns an aligned feature frame, │
│                          │ and `run_experiment(...)` returns the same dictionary with train/test/val predictions and │
│                          │ the fitted model.                                                                         │
