╭──────────────────────────┬───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────╮
│ Field                    │ Value                                                                                     │
├──────────────────────────┼───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────┤
│ patch_type               │ diff                                                                                      │
│ patch_name               │ add_cross_sectional_rank_features                                                         │
│ patch_description        │ The current program achieves a slope_ratio of 19.9%, which is the best so far, but still  │
│                          │ far from the target of 25%+. The primary issue is that features are regime-specific -     │
│                          │ they work well in training but degrade in validation.                                     │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The recommendation suggests adding cross-sectional rank features within rolling windows   │
│                          │ to enhance regime invariance. While the current program already has `vol_rank_60`, we     │
│                          │ should extend this ranking approach to other key features. Ranking transforms absolute    │
│                          │ values into percentile positions within a rolling window, making them                     │
│                          │ distribution-invariant across regime shifts.                                              │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ I will add three new rank-based features:                                                 │
│                          │ 1. `ret_3_rank_40` - ranks 3-period returns within a 40-bar window                        │
│                          │ 2. `efficiency_10_rank_60` - ranks 10-period efficiency within a 60-bar window            │
│                          │ 3. `rsi_14_rank_40` - ranks RSI(14) within a 40-bar window                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ These features should be more stable across regime shifts than their raw counterparts     │
│                          │ because they measure relative position rather than absolute level. A 1% return might be   │
│                          │ extreme in low-vol regimes but normal in high-vol regimes - the rank captures this        │
│                          │ context automatically.                                                                    │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ The window sizes (40-60 bars) are chosen to be:                                           │
│                          │ - Long enough to capture meaningful distribution shifts                                   │
│                          │ - Short enough to stay responsive to recent market character                              │
│                          │ - Aligned with the successful `vol_rank_60` already in use                                │
│                          │                                                                                           │
│                          │ This maintains the 80-feature limit while adding genuinely regime-invariant signals that  │
│                          │ should improve the slope_ratio without sacrificing the excellent generalization_ratio     │
│                          │ (96.0%).                                                                                  │
